      products: { status: "PROVEN", evidence: "P197-B (2026-08-24): the ladder now runs on its own schedule — the one reason this stage stayed PARTIAL — with multiple scheduled-run receipts (gtp-bot matchweek builds at 14:47, 15:15 and 21:28 on 08-23 alone) and an ACTIVE, dated receipt for the 08-29 slate on the control plane. Everything else was already proven when the lane opened (P189-B): selection on the market's own price with a guard that fails if the builder reads a forecast artifact, legs settleable end to end through gradeEplLeg, bands from the canonical bucket with unreachable ones reported at the prices they actually reached, and the four defects building it surfaced each pinned by a guard. Rides the shared risk-substitute owner (P196-B2)." },
      publication: { status: "PROVEN", evidence: "P185 (2026-08-20): /epl derives its freshness from the forecast artifact's OWN stamp — the built export renders \"Generated 2026-08-20T23:21:41Z\", byte-identical to generatedAt in the EPL lane's own forecasts/latest.json artifact, never a build time or a hand-typed date. Coverage is published without rounding up: 9 rows CURRENT_PRE_EVENT with per-fixture distributions, and the 1 fixture the provider returned no market for is NAMED on the page (\"Not forecast: ... READY_EXCEPT_ODDS\") rather than dropped to make 9 of 10 look like 10 of 10. The limitation travels WITH the numbers in rendered words: \"not validated out of sample\" as the page heading, and \"no Premier League match has been graded under this model ... no track record to cite\" above the table — so publication cannot be read as a claim of accuracy. Newly-promoted clubs on the league-average baseline are flagged inline per row, not silently priced. PROMOTED ONLY AFTER two contradictions were removed, both of which had a public surface asserting the opposite of the page: the nav entry read \"Schedule · simulation pending\" and rendered ON /epl directly above that heading, and the shared schedule component hardcoded \"the sports that are simulated today are MLB and NFL\" while /simulate derived 1. The two guards that should have caught the nav state were passing VACUOUSLY — they matched the schedule-only sentence inside the shared component, which /epl reaches only on the FALSE branch of a ternary it passes true; both now assert against the built export. This stage is about the publication path only: calibration and settlement remain separate stages and are NOT implied by it. P189 UPDATE (2026-08-21): the track-record sentence was a HARD-CODED string reading \"No Premier League match has been graded under this model\" — true when written, false the night Arsenal v Coventry City settled, in an artifact a settler was quietly invalidating. It is now derived from the ledger and reads \"1 Premier League match has been graded ... far too few to support any accuracy claim\". The derivation keeps it honest in BOTH directions: it cannot understate a growing record, and it refuses to quote an accuracy figure at any sample size, because a hit rate over a handful of matches is noise with a percent sign on it. validation stays NOT_VALIDATED_OUT_OF_SAMPLE regardless of count — no number on that line is a validation. The page's unreadable-artifact FALLBACK carried the same false sentence and now says the record could not be read, which is a statement about our files rather than about the world. EXTENDED same day (P188): publication now covers a per-fixture report at /epl/match/[slug], statically generated for each priced fixture from the same artifact through one shared loader, so the hub, the fixture list and the report cannot disagree about a fixture. The reports publish what the engine already computed and had been discarding — the ten likeliest scorelines with the mass they account for, the goal-total distribution and its over/under ladder, each side's own goal curve, both-teams-to-score, clean sheets and the winning-margin distribution — every figure an exact sum over ONE Poisson matrix, which is why NO run count is quoted anywhere for this sport: nothing is sampled. The workflow's produced-artifact assertion was extended to the PUBLIC forecast file at the same time; it had checked only the private research copy, so a run that wrote one and not the other would have reported success while every reader-facing surface went stale." },
