      markets: { status: "PROVEN", evidence: "AUTHORIZED 2026-08-18 by a committed founder receipt (docs/receipts/ODDS_AUTHORIZATION_UFC.md): UFC/MMA only, h2h only, us region only, BULK endpoint only, 500-credit ceiling, its own ledger. Parsed fail-closed in BOTH directions — the NFL receipt cannot fund a fight card and this one cannot fund an NFL slate (guard lib/sports/odds/ufc-authorization.test.mjs, 11 checks incl. two-key widening, ceiling-crossing refusal, per-event-endpoint ban). PROVEN 2026-08-20 by run 32421589470 — the first run to satisfy this stage's ACTUAL proof, de-vigged implied probabilities rather than merely a successful call. Capture: 94 per-book h2h rows across 12 bouts from 8 bookmakers, 1 credit (cumulative 5 of 500). Consumption: the preflight read that snapshot and priced 6 bouts CURRENT_PRE_EVENT carrying impliedSum + noVig per bookmaker; 6 ABSTAIN on model grounds with stated reasons; 1 READY_EXCEPT_ODDS — Spivac vs Petrino, EXACTLY the bout the capture independently reported under unjoinedBouts. The two artifacts agree about which fight has no market, which is what separates a working join from one that merely returns rows. Until this run the de-vig path was reachable from nothing: capture green, code committed and unit-tested, and the only caller ever passing oddsSnapshot was a test — so oddsReady:true meant prices joined to bouts, never that probabilities existed" },
      schedule: { status: "PROVEN", evidence: "P197-A (2026-08-24): population-exact against the provider's OWN event, on a schedule. Daily capture in sport-schedules (dated capture files = cadence receipts, plus the always-committed cadence receipt from P196-F), and audit-ufc-card-population re-fetches the event with limit=1000 and reconciles expected/captured/read/priced/missing/phantom — receipt data/internal/research/ufc/population/2026-08-29.json: 13/13, 0 missing, 0 phantom. The audit runs inside ufc-fight-week after every card rebuild and FAILS the job on mismatch (exit 2), because the 08-22 card shipped 7 of 13 bouts looking complete; a guard scans every scoreboard call for the limit parameter. Slate dates are the event's own, so a card running past midnight UTC stays on its own day." },
