      monitoring: { status: "PROVEN", evidence: "P197-A (2026-08-24): the lane's chain is covered by named instruments with owners and remediation. build-ufc-lane-status (INTERNAL_ADMIN, fight-week cadence) carries per-artifact freshness from each artifact's own stamp, the POPULATION receipt state (POPULATION_EXACT/MISMATCH with counts), the model-vs-market block (cumulative record, per-card frozen=graded+void+pending reconciliation RECONCILED/DRIFT, post-card grading lag CLEAR/PENDING_RESULTS), credit spend against the 500-credit ceiling, and the gate's own remaining path. The evidence ledger derives its sport row from the twelve-stage gate, never hand-written; cron-slot-watchdog covers the scheduled jobs; the population audit fails the fight-week job on mismatch rather than warning." },
      settlement: { status: "PROVEN", evidence: "P197-A (2026-08-24): official, exactly-once, and RECONCILED — proven live on a real card. Two independent official sources merge with refuse-on-conflict (zero conflicts across 1,574 overlapping bouts); the graded ledger is append-only with a bout scored exactly once; draws/no-contests VOID rather than counting either way; every graded row records which source supplied its outcome. Per-event reconciliation (frozen = graded + void + pending, from the snapshots and the ledger, never memory) is on the summary and the lane status: 08-22 closed 10 = 10 + 0 + 0 after the truncation and rollover fixes graded the main card; 08-29 is frozen 6, all pending pre-card. The post-card job refreshes the free results capture in-job before grading, so a Saturday card grades Sunday morning, not Monday." },
      data: { status: "PROVEN", evidence: "P197-A (2026-08-24): a typed per-bout INPUT MATRIX on the population receipt — fighter history AVAILABLE for every read bout and SPARSE (named, from the model card's own abstention) for the three unmodelled ones; market price AVAILABLE/MISSING per bout from the pre-fight snapshot; weigh-ins, replacements and physicals typed UNSUPPORTED because no licensed per-card source exists — absence is a state, never a zero. All inputs are free (research corpus + ESPN public endpoints); the only paid input is the odds receipt under its own guarded budget (9/500 credits). The third-party corpus runs ~8 days behind and is mitigated by the same-day ESPN capture in the dual-source merge — stated, not solved." },
      calibration: { status: "UNPROVEN", evidence: "P191 (2026-08-22): THE INSTRUMENT NOW EXISTS; THE SAMPLE DOES NOT. This stage read as a small-sample problem, and it was not — every ingredient had been on disk the whole time and nothing joined them. The model publishes a winner probability for each bout, the provider posts both sides' prices, and official results are captured after the card; no code had ever put the three together, so \"we have never measured this\" was being filed as \"we cannot measure this\". lib/sports/ufc/model-vs-market.mjs de-vigs the two-way price by proportional normalisation (refusing any pair whose implied sum falls outside 1.00-1.25, which is the shape of a mispaired or stale quote rather than a book's margin) and scores the model AND the market by log loss and Brier over exactly the same bouts. BOTH PROBABILITIES ARE RECORDED BEFORE THE CARD, in an immutable stamped snapshot that refuses to write once the first bout has started. That ordering is the whole point: a comparison assembled afterwards from whatever odds file is on disk scores the model against a price that did not exist when it spoke, which is how EPL lost its baseline on the one match where model and market sharply disagreed. A bout is scored only when BOTH sides have something to say - a missing price is a gap in our capture and a missing read is the model abstaining, and neither is evidence about the other - and a draw or no-contest is VOIDED rather than counted as a miss, because the model answered who wins and the fight produced no winner. Live: the 2026-08-22 card is captured, 10 bouts priced and read, 3 skipped and named. The disagreement is large - +30.0pp on Gauge Young, +28.1pp on Roman Dolidze, -20.5pp on Carli Judice, and 7 of 10 model probabilities above the market's for the same fighter - which is a model making a big claim rather than a model that is right. ufc-post-card, which was DISPATCH-ONLY AND HAD NEVER RUN, now grades this on a daily cron; until that ran, nothing captured a finished card at all. UNPROVEN and NOT PROMOTABLE BY ENGINEERING: one card is not a calibration, the grader says so in its own output, and this stage stays here until enough bouts have been fought. REALITY_GATED, not backlog." },
      model: { status: "PARTIAL", evidence: "Program 153: abstaining Elo baseline — coverage 25.6% BY DESIGN (in-corpus history only, stated), Elo 0.6791 vs listing-order prior 0.6906 vs coin ln(2) over 289 covered bouts; last-card replay through the shared harness (5/12 covered, abstentions with reasons ON the artifact); model-card-v1 names the weak signal as the finding. The BAR a future fight model must beat; activation OFF. P167-F: the abstaining Elo promoted to the live adapter (coverage semantics preserved: 288/1133 covered, logLoss 0.6788, ECE 0.0157, abstention 843 SPARSE/2 IDLE), per-bout ladder with CARD_UNCERTAIN lineage gate; REAL UFC 330 preflight: 11 READY_EXCEPT_ODDS + 1 ABSTAIN(SPARSE) (data/internal/research/ufc/model-card-v2.json) P190 UPDATE (2026-08-22): THIS ENTRY DESCRIBED A SUPERSEDED MODEL. Everything above is the abstaining Elo; what has actually been publishing on /ufc is a three-head fight model — winner, method of victory and ending round for every bout — fitted on 8,642 decisive bouts with each head gated separately against a base-rate baseline. On a 3,557-fight chronological holdout all three PASS: winner gain 0.0317 at 60.6% accuracy against a coin, method gain 0.0563, round gain 0.0401, max calibration z 0.876 against a bar of 2. The leakage discipline is real and was verified rather than assumed: features accumulate by replaying fights in DATE ORDER so a bout sees only what happened strictly before it, the holdout is the later slice and never a random split, Platt is fitted on an INNER fold because a calibrator fitted on the slice it will be applied to learns the identity, and the evaluator and the card builder import the SAME library so the thing validated and the thing published cannot drift. TWO PROVENANCE DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED. The published model id was the literal \"ufc-fight-v1\", which would have read v1 however far the model drifted; it is now derived from the model's own definition (feature sets, method vocabulary, bars) so adding a feature changes it without anyone remembering to, and it is carried on every card and every paper-card leg so a settled row can name the model that produced it. And the committed evaluation was computed from DIFFERENT CODE than was running: lib/fight-model.mjs was edited eighteen minutes after the evaluation was last written and never re-run, so for four days /ufc showed PASS verdicts from one model beside predictions from another. Re-running moved the winner gain from 0.0147 to 0.0317 — an improvement, which is luck; the same silence would have hidden a regression. The evaluation now records a hash of the library's own bytes and the card builder REFUSES to publish when they differ, naming both hashes and the one-command remedy. The definition fingerprint could not have caught it (the change was a NaN fix touching no feature and no bar) and the source hash would churn on a refit, so both instruments are kept. A model card for the deployed model now exists at data/internal/research/ufc/model-card-fight-v1.json, every figure derived from the evaluation rather than transcribed; the two pre-existing cards both described the Elo. PARTIAL, not PROVEN: every gain above is measured against a BASE RATE, not against a price, so nothing here supports a claim about a market. P191 built the missing comparison (see the calibration stage) and captured the first card, but a captured card is not a scored one — this stays PARTIAL until bouts have been fought and graded against the de-vigged line." },
